Q: EventListener add and remove not working correctly A: <p>The function you are removing is not the same function you added. The only way to remove a listener is to give it the same function that is already added as a listener. For example:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function updateTextClickListener(evt) {
  console.log('event click here')
}

function installUpdateTextClickListener() {
  var btn = document.getElementById('updateText');
  btn.addEventListener('click', updateTextClickListener, true)
}    
function uninstallUpdateTextClickListener() {
    var btn = document.getElementById('updateText');
    btn.removeEventListener('click', updateTextClickListener, true)
}
window.onload = function(){
  installUpdateTextClickListener();

  setTimeout(function(){
    uninstallUpdateTextClickListener();
  }, 2000)
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button id="updateText"&gt;Update Text&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Note that if you create an identical function, it will still not work, because &quot;identical&quot; is not &quot;same&quot;:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const first  = function() { return 42; }
const second = function() { return 42; }

console.log(first === first)
// true
console.log(first === second)
// false</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How to display data in view of the CodeIgniter? A: <p>Try this:</p>
<p>Model</p>
<pre><code>public function get_builders()  
{  
    $query1 = $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;select('name')
        -&gt;from('builders')
        -&gt;like('name', 'a')
        -&gt;or_like('name', 'b')
        -&gt;limit(3)
        -&gt;get();

    $result = $query1-&gt;result();
    return $result;
} 
</code></pre>
<p>View</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php foreach ($h as $row) { ?&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt; 
        &lt;td&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#&quot;&gt;&lt;?php echo $row-&gt;name; ?&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt; 
    &lt;/tr&gt;  
&lt;?php } ?&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How to go from a std::vector or std::deque to a std::initiailzer_list? A: <p><strong>The length of a init-list is determined at compile time.</strong> This can't be bypassed since it's an API constraint of the boost function.</p>
<p>Another problem is that an init-list is just a view <strong>over a hidden array</strong>, not even over the objects that were passed to the constructor to create the init-list (so it doesn't matter if those objects were thin views themselves or what their lifetime was). That means you can't construct a <code>std::initializer_list</code> object in a function and return it from that function - the lifetime of its hidden backing array doesn't extend past the return. For example this is invalid:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto create_args(const std::string_view &amp;a, const std::string_view &amp;b) {
    return std::initializer_list{a, b};
}
</code></pre>
<p>It is, however, possible to use template metaprogramming to write a wrapper call that constructs the init-list in place. This example works for any container with <code>operator[]</code> but, as mentioned to be unavoidable above, requires the number of args to be a compile-time constant explicitly passed to the call.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;deque&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

void f(std::initializer_list&lt;std::string_view&gt; args);

template &lt;class C, std::size_t... I&gt;
auto call_f_internal(const C &amp;args, std::index_sequence&lt;I...&gt;) {
    return f({std::string_view{args[I]}...});
}

template &lt;std::size_t N, class C&gt;
auto f(const C &amp;args) {
    return call_f_internal(args, std::make_index_sequence&lt;N&gt;{});
}

int main() {
    std::deque&lt;std::string&gt; args;
    args.emplace_back(&quot;ab&quot;);
    args.emplace_back(&quot;bcd&quot;);

    f&lt;2&gt;(args);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The best solution, of course, is an API that doesn't require an initializer list unless it's truly the intent to have the caller pass explicit lists of arguments at the point of call. If you're really at Boost v2, the <a href="https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/latest/libs/process/doc/html/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">documentation</a> offers an overload that doesn't require an init-list:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>  // Construct a child from a property list and launch it using the default launcher..
  template&lt;typename Args, typename ... Inits&gt;
  explicit basic_process(
      executor_type executor,
      const filesystem::path&amp; exe,
      Args&amp;&amp; args, Inits&amp;&amp;... inits);
</code></pre>
<p>Other than the signature, this type of overloads is <em>woefully</em> undocumented, but it accepts a variable of type <code>std::deque&lt;std::string&gt;</code> and similar.</p>
